<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:sm="using:CircuitRF.Ui.Smith"
             xmlns:ctl="using:CircuitRF.Ui.Controls"
             xmlns:ddc="using:CircuitRF.Ui.DataDisplay.Controls"
             xmlns:ddv="using:CircuitRF.Ui.DataDisplay.ViewModels"
             xmlns:ddvw="using:CircuitRF.Ui.Views.DataDisplay"
             xmlns:sv="using:CircuitRF.Ui.Views.Smith"
             xmlns:mi="clr-namespace:Material.Icons.Avalonia;assembly=Material.Icons.Avalonia"
             x:Class="CircuitRF.Ui.Views.Smith.SmithChartView"
             x:DataType="sm:SmithChartDocument"
             Background="{DynamicResource SystemChromeLowColor}">

    <!-- ══ The Smith Chart document's window ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         brief-smith-4-document-window.md R-smith4-3/-4/-5/-7/-8; docs/design/smith-chart.md §5.2/§5.3.

         THREE REGIONS AND A STATUS STRIP, in the proportions §5.2's sketch sets: a narrow generator
         column on the left, the chart taking the large majority of the area because THE CHART IS THE
         TOOL, the network strip below it, and a strip across the foot that states numbers. The two
         splitters are draggable and their positions persist in the document's own View block.

         THE CHART PANE IS BRIEF 5's and is a real PlotControl; THE NETWORK PANE IS BRIEF 6's and is a
         real SchematicRenderer. Neither is a second renderer: the chart is a Data Display Plot and the
         network is a projection onto the schematic editor's own read model, so the only thing this
         window draws itself is its chrome. R-smith4-1's whole claim is that the shell around them is
         inherited rather than written — there is no Window subclass anywhere in this folder, and if one ever appears,
         that requirement has been missed.

         THE CHROME IS BORROWED AND NOT INVENTED (R-smith4-4). Border.pane / Border.card, sentence-case
         headings, label left and value right in one right-aligned column, centred text on buttons and
         comboboxes, compact sliders, a status strip with numbers in it. match.md §9 and railrf.md
         §11.1-§11.2 settled all of it over roughly ten rounds of owner review; MatchDesignerWindow is
         the reference and these styles are transcribed from it rather than re-decided.

         EVERY EDITABLE VALUE IS AN InlineEditText (R-smith4-5, owner instruction, and the word is
         every). The generator table's f, R and X cells; the chart Z0; the design frequency. Brief 6's
         slider values and element names and brief 9's sweep and Q rows join them under the same rule.
         There is deliberately NO bare TextBox bound two-way to a design value in this folder, and the
         gate scans for one. Do not write a fourth inline editor here; that is the trap the rule
         closes.

         THE HOSTING IS THE IN-PLACE ONE (R-smith4-6). The generator table is the only grid of cells in
         this window and its three columns are fixed-width by construction — a frequency and two ohm
         values — so the box swaps in place and nothing moves. Nothing in this series needs the
         floating Canvas overlay, which is the more delicate of the two; if it starts to look
         necessary, the column widths are the thing to fix.
    ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <UserControl.Styles>
        <!-- ── The panel language, transcribed from MatchDesignerWindow ──────────────────────────
             Two tiers, and the whole point is that they are DIFFERENT: a pane is
             SystemChromeMediumLowColor, a card inside it SystemChromeLowColor, so a group reads as a
             thing sitting ON the panel rather than as a rectangle drawn on it. -->
        <Style Selector="Border.pane">
            <Setter Property="Background"      Value="{DynamicResource SystemChromeMediumLowColor}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"     Value="{DynamicResource CrfTileBorderBrush}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius"    Value="6"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"         Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"          Value="4"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Border.card">
            <Setter Property="Background"      Value="{DynamicResource SystemChromeLowColor}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"     Value="{DynamicResource CrfTileBorderBrush}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius"    Value="6"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"         Value="8,5"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"          Value="0,0,0,5"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- Sentence case, never shouted (owner, on the Match Designer, same round). -->
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.panelhdr">
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"     Value="0,0,0,6"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.cardhdr">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"   Value="11"/>
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"     Value="0,0,0,2"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.detailLabel">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"          Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="Opacity"           Value="0.6"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector=":is(TextBlock).note">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"     Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="Opacity"      Value="0.75"/>
            <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector=":is(TextBlock).warn">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"     Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground"   Value="{DynamicResource CrfWarningBrush}"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- A click-to-sort-style column header stays LEFT-aligned with its column — the one standing
             exception to "centred text", and the reason is that the text under it starts at the
             column's own left edge, so any centring here is a misalignment the user can see. These
             three are not sortable (the table is sorted by frequency by construction), but they are
             column headers and they follow the column-header rule. -->
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.gridhdr">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"            Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="FontWeight"          Value="SemiBold"/>
            <Setter Property="Opacity"             Value="0.7"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- A settable value in a label-left/value-right row: the numbers form ONE right-aligned
             column whether the row is settable or read-only. -->
        <Style Selector="ctl|InlineEditText.val">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"                   Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Right"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- A DISABLED settable value has to LOOK disabled, and nothing else in the stack makes it:
             an InlineEditText at rest is a bare TextBlock, and Avalonia's Fluent theme dims neither a
             disabled TextBlock nor a disabled Panel, so a field that is switched off reads exactly
             like one that is on and simply swallows the double-click. -->
        <Style Selector="ctl|InlineEditText:disabled">
            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.45"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- THE OFFENDING INPUT TURNS RED (§5.3). The strip's sentence names the span with its numbers
             in it; this is the other half of that one mechanism, so the reader does not have to hunt
             for which field the sentence is about. -->
        <Style Selector="ctl|InlineEditText.bad">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource CrfWarningBrush}"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- Centred text on buttons (§5.3). -->
        <Style Selector="Button.panel">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"                    Value="11"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"                     Value="6,3"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment"  Value="Center"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment"         Value="Stretch"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.tiny">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"                   Value="11"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"                    Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Width"                      Value="22"/>
            <Setter Property="Height"                     Value="20"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment"   Value="Center"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- The generator table virtualizes and reads as rows rather than as a pick list — the same
             answer TechEditorView's row lists and the Match Designer's solutions list both settled
             on, including the class name. -->
        <Style Selector="ListBox.rows">
            <Setter Property="Background"      Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"         Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility" Value="Disabled"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="ListBox.rows ListBoxItem">
            <Setter Property="Padding"                    Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin"                     Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="MinHeight"                  Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="ListBox.rows ListBoxItem /template/ ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- Selection-dependent buttons dim their ICON when disabled — the schematic editor's own
             style, and the reason it exists there: a MaterialIcon inside a disabled Button is not
             dimmed by Fluent, so a button that cannot act reads exactly like one that can. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.SelectionBtn:disabled /template/ ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.35"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- A toolbar button that is ON — the schematic and layout editors' own ToolActive, taken
             verbatim so the constant-Q button reads the way every other latched tool in circuitRF
             does. It is what replaces the "Show arcs" checkbox the panel used to carry. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.ToolActive">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="White"/>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.ToolActive /template/ ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <SolidColorBrush Color="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColor}"/>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.ToolActive:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <SolidColorBrush Color="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColorLight1}"/>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>
        <Style Selector="Button.ToolActive:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <SolidColorBrush Color="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColorDark1}"/>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- A slider row's parameter label. It is a BUTTON because clicking it does something —
             it makes that parameter the one the chart's gripper drags (R-smith6-5) — and it is
             chromeless because a row of three raised buttons beside three sliders would read as the
             control rather than as the label it is. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.rowlabel">
            <Setter Property="FontSize"                   Value="10"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding"                    Value="3,1"/>
            <Setter Property="MinHeight"                  Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="MinWidth"                   Value="0"/>
            <Setter Property="Background"                 Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness"            Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush"                Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        </Style>
        <!-- THE ACTIVE ROW IS MARKED. Without it the gripper on the chart silently changes what it
             does, and the only way to find out which parameter it is on is to drag it. -->
        <Style Selector="Button.rowlabel.activeparam">
            <Setter Property="FontWeight"  Value="SemiBold"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColor}"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- Compact sliders (§5.3) — no explicit Height, which would squash the thumb off-centre; a
             negative vertical margin that pulls the layout footprint back in. Declared here rather
             than in brief 6 so the strip's rows are the same height whoever adds the first slider. -->
        <Style Selector="Slider.compact">
            <Setter Property="Margin"            Value="4,-7"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        </Style>
    </UserControl.Styles>

    <!-- ── The two document-wide keys (owner instruction, 2026-09-19) ─────────────────────────────
         DELETE removes the selected MARKERS and nothing else — deliberately not the Data Display's
         own DeleteSelectedCommand, which also removes selected plot containers and this document's
         one chart is not deletable. ESCAPE drops both selections, the marker's and the network
         strip's element.

         On the ROOT, so they work wherever the focus is inside the document — and the two surfaces
         that want either key for themselves already take it before it gets here: the network canvas
         handles Delete as "delete the selected element", and PlotControl consumes Escape while an
         overlay drag is in flight (R-smith5-8). A KeyBinding only ever sees the keys neither of them
         marked handled. -->
    <UserControl.KeyBindings>
        <KeyBinding Gesture="Delete" Command="{Binding ViewModel.DeleteSelectedMarkersCommand}"/>
        <KeyBinding Gesture="Back"   Command="{Binding ViewModel.DeleteSelectedMarkersCommand}"/>
        <KeyBinding Gesture="Escape" Command="{Binding ViewModel.ClearSelectionCommand}"/>
    </UserControl.KeyBindings>

    <!-- The two splits are the document's own View block, applied and written back in CODE rather
         than bound: a RowDefinition is not in the logical tree, inherits no DataContext, and a
         binding on its Height resolves against nothing — silently, with the splitter simply not
         remembering anything. See SmithChartView.ApplySplitFractions.

         A drag is NOT an edit: it pushes no undo entry and raises no dirty mark. -->
    <Grid Name="RootGrid">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <!-- Row 0 the chart, row 2 the network strip. Named through the GRID and reached by
                 index from the code-behind: x:Name on a definition generates no field, because a
                 definition is not a control and is not in the name scope. -->
            <RowDefinition Height="0.65*" MinHeight="120"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="0.35*" MinHeight="80"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- ══ Top region: the generator column and the chart ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="0" Name="TopRegion">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <!-- NARROWER THAN §5.2's SKETCH (owner instruction, 2026-09-19): the space it
                     gives back is the chart's, and the chart is where marker info boxes are parked.
                     The minimum is what the three fixed-width table columns plus their padding
                     actually need — below it the frequency cells start to clip, which the in-place
                     inline editor has no way to report. -->
                <ColumnDefinition Width="0.16*" MinWidth="176"/>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="0.84*" MinWidth="200"/>
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

            <!-- ── The generator panel (R-smith4-7) ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
            <Border Grid.Column="0" Classes="pane">
                <ScrollViewer HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
                    <StackPanel>
                        <TextBlock Classes="panelhdr" Text="Generator"/>

                        <!-- The table. THREE FIXED-WIDTH COLUMNS, which is what makes R-smith4-6's
                             in-place hosting the right one: a frequency and two ohm values, and no
                             column here is width-shared with anything. -->
                        <Border Classes="card">
                            <StackPanel>
                                <!-- A HEADER SITS OVER ITS OWN COLUMN, which means it takes that
                                     column's alignment and its margin rather than gridhdr's own.
                                     R and X are right-aligned numbers, so left-aligned headers put
                                     "X" directly above the R column's digits and "R" above nothing
                                     at all - two ohm values one place out of step, which is a wrong
                                     impedance read off a table that looks entirely ordinary
                                     (R-smith11-4). The three Margin/alignment pairs below are the
                                     row template's, copied cell for cell. -->
                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="86,*,*" Margin="0,0,0,2">
                                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="gridhdr" Text="f"/>
                                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Classes="gridhdr" Text="R" Margin="4,0,0,0"
                                               HorizontalAlignment="Right"/>
                                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Classes="gridhdr" Text="X" Margin="4,0,0,0"
                                               HorizontalAlignment="Right"/>
                                </Grid>

                                <ListBox Classes="rows" MaxHeight="190"
                                         ItemsSource="{Binding ViewModel.GeneratorRows}"
                                         SelectedItem="{Binding ViewModel.SelectedRow, Mode=TwoWay}">
                                    <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                        <DataTemplate x:DataType="sm:SmithGeneratorRowViewModel">
                                            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="86,*,*" Margin="0,1">
                                                <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="0" Classes="val"
                                                                    HorizontalContentAlignment="Left"
                                                                    Text="{Binding FrequencyEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                                    ToolTip.Tip="Double-click to edit. Type a unit (2, 2 GHz, 1800 MHz) — the number is pre-selected and the unit is left alone. Rows are kept sorted by frequency."/>
                                                <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val" Margin="4,0,0,0"
                                                                    Text="{Binding ResistanceEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                                    ToolTip.Tip="The generator's resistance at this frequency. Double-click to edit."/>
                                                <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="2" Classes="val" Margin="4,0,0,0"
                                                                    Text="{Binding ReactanceEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                                    ToolTip.Tip="The generator's reactance at this frequency — negative is the ordinary case. Double-click to edit."/>
                                            </Grid>
                                        </DataTemplate>
                                    </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                </ListBox>

                                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4" Margin="0,4,0,0"
                                            HorizontalAlignment="Right">
                                    <Button Classes="tiny" Content="+"
                                            Command="{Binding ViewModel.AddGeneratorRowCommand}"
                                            ToolTip.Tip="Add a row, above the top of the table so it does not collide with one that is already there."/>
                                    <!-- The last row cannot be removed: an empty generator table is a
                                         refusal, so a button that could produce one would only ever
                                         break the document. -->
                                    <Button Classes="tiny" Content="−"
                                            Command="{Binding ViewModel.RemoveGeneratorRowCommand}"
                                            ToolTip.Tip="Remove the selected row. The last row stays — this tool starts from an impedance, so the table is never empty."/>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Border>

                        <!-- Conjugate / Import / Re-import.

                             CONJUGATE FIRST (owner instruction, 2026-09-19). It acts on the table
                             that is directly above it, so it sits next to it; the import is what
                             REPLACES that table, and it belongs below with the path it read from
                             rather than between the table and the one button that edits it. -->
                        <Border Classes="card">
                            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
                                <Button Classes="panel" Content="Conjugate"
                                        Command="{Binding ViewModel.ConjugateCommand}"
                                        ToolTip.Tip="Negate every row's reactance, once. One undo entry, and not a persistent flag — pressing it twice puts the table back."/>

                                <Button Classes="panel" Name="ImportS1pButton" Content="Import .s1p…"
                                        Click="OnImportS1pClick"
                                        ToolTip.Tip="Read a one-port Touchstone file. Its rows REPLACE the table, and the path is recorded as provenance only — the numbers are copied in, so this document stays portable if the file moves."/>

                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
                                    <!-- Provenance, not a reference. Nothing resolves this at load, which
                                         is why a moved file cannot stop a document opening. -->
                                    <TextBlock Classes="note" Grid.Column="0" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                                               TextWrapping="NoWrap" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                               Text="{Binding ViewModel.SourcePathDisplay}"
                                               IsVisible="{Binding ViewModel.HasSourcePath}"
                                               ToolTip.Tip="{Binding ViewModel.SourcePathDisplay}"/>
                                    <Button Grid.Column="1" Classes="panel" Content="Re-import"
                                            Margin="4,0,0,0"
                                            IsVisible="{Binding ViewModel.HasSourcePath}"
                                            Command="{Binding ViewModel.ReimportGeneratorCommand}"
                                            ToolTip.Tip="Repeat the read from the file above."/>
                                </Grid>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Border>

                        <!-- Label left, value right, in one right-aligned column. -->
                        <Border Classes="card">
                            <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Chart"/>

                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel" Text="Z₀"/>
                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val" Margin="4,0,0,0"
                                                        Text="{Binding ViewModel.ChartZ0Entry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                        ToolTip.Tip="One real, document-wide reference impedance. Γ is taken against it and every overlay is renormalized to it. Double-click to edit."/>
                                </Grid>

                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel" Text="Design f"/>
                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val" Margin="4,0,0,0"
                                                        Classes.bad="{Binding ViewModel.IsDesignFrequencyInvalid}"
                                                        Text="{Binding ViewModel.DesignFrequencyEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                        ToolTip.Tip="What the trajectories are drawn at and what the strip reports. It need not be a row of the table, but it must lie inside the table's span — the generator impedance is interpolated between rows, never extrapolated past them."/>
                                </Grid>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Border>

                        <!-- ── The overlays (brief 8) ───────────────────────────────────────
                             R-smith8-1/-2/-3/-4; docs/design/smith-chart.md §5.7.

                             REFERENCE MATERIAL UNDER THE WORK, and none of it is part of the
                             cascade: an overlay is not an element, it is not in the node array, it
                             is not in the network strip, and it stays out of the chart's autoscale
                             unless its own row says otherwise — a stability circle can be enormous
                             and one unlucky overlay must not reframe the work.

                             EVERY ROW IS AN ORDINARY DATA DISPLAY TRACE. The quantity cell takes
                             what a trace card takes — S11, S21, Z11, a cube slice — and the derived
                             cell takes a DerivedParameters name, of which SourceStabilityCircle and
                             LoadStabilityCircle are the two this tool was asked for. Nothing here
                             computes any of it.

                             A ROW THAT DOES NOT RESOLVE IS MARKED AND SAYS WHY, and the document
                             still opens with the rest of the chart drawn. That is the opposite of
                             an S1P ELEMENT, whose missing file is a refusal, and the difference is
                             the point: the cascade cannot be walked without an element, and an
                             overlay's absence costs the user a comparison and nothing else. -->
                        <Border Classes="card">
                            <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Overlays"/>

                                <TextBlock Classes="note" IsVisible="{Binding !ViewModel.HasOverlays}"
                                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                           Text="Reference data under the work — a Touchstone file, or a cube in an open data set. Everything is renormalized to the chart's Z₀ on the way in."/>

                                <ListBox Classes="rows" MaxHeight="150"
                                         IsVisible="{Binding ViewModel.HasOverlays}"
                                         ItemsSource="{Binding ViewModel.OverlayRows}"
                                         SelectedItem="{Binding ViewModel.SelectedOverlay, Mode=TwoWay}">
                                    <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                        <DataTemplate x:DataType="sm:SmithOverlayRowViewModel">
                                            <StackPanel Margin="0,1" Spacing="1">
                                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                                    <CheckBox Grid.Column="0" Padding="4,0,0,0" MinHeight="0"
                                                              IsChecked="{Binding Visible, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                              ToolTip.Tip="Draw this overlay. A hidden row is not on the chart at all, so it is also out of the trace list and the Add Marker menu."/>
                                                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                                                               Classes.bad="{Binding IsUnresolved}"
                                                               Text="{Binding Label}"
                                                               ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Unresolved}"/>
                                                </Grid>

                                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" Margin="20,0,0,0">
                                                    <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel" Text="Quantity"/>
                                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val" Margin="4,0,0,0"
                                                                        Text="{Binding QuantityEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                                        ToolTip.Tip="S11, S21, Z11, Y21 — a matrix letter and a pair of 1-BASED port numbers — or a cube slice. Double-click to edit."/>
                                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="2" Classes="val" Margin="6,0,0,0"
                                                                        Text="{Binding DerivedEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                                        ToolTip.Tip="A derived quantity, or None. SourceStabilityCircle and LoadStabilityCircle are drawn as circles in the Γ plane; a derived row ignores the quantity beside it."/>
                                                </Grid>

                                                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8" Margin="20,0,0,0">
                                                    <CheckBox MinHeight="0" FontSize="11" Content="Renormalize"
                                                              IsChecked="{Binding Renormalize, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                              ToolTip.Tip="Renormalize to the chart's Z₀ on the way in. ON is the right answer: a 75 Ω part drawn on a 50 Ω chart without it is a curve in the wrong place that looks entirely plausible."/>
                                                    <CheckBox MinHeight="0" FontSize="11" Content="Autoscale"
                                                              IsChecked="{Binding IncludeInAutoscale, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                              ToolTip.Tip="Let this row take part in the chart's autoscale. Off by default — a stability circle can be enormous, and one unlucky overlay would squash the cascade into a corner."/>
                                                </StackPanel>
                                            </StackPanel>
                                        </DataTemplate>
                                    </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                </ListBox>

                                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4" Margin="0,4,0,0"
                                            HorizontalAlignment="Right">
                                    <Button Classes="tiny" Content="+"
                                            Command="{Binding ViewModel.AddOverlayCommand}"
                                            ToolTip.Tip="Add a Touchstone file as an overlay. It is REFERENCED by a path relative to this document, never copied into it — an overlay is reference material, so a stale copy would be the wrong answer."/>
                                    <Button Classes="tiny" Content="−"
                                            Command="{Binding ViewModel.RemoveOverlayCommand}"
                                            ToolTip.Tip="Remove the selected overlay. Nothing else changes — an overlay is not part of the cascade."/>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Border>

                        <!-- ── The swept band (brief 9) ─────────────────────────────────────
                             R-smith9-4; docs/design/smith-chart.md §3.6.

                             OPTIONAL AND OFF BY DEFAULT (owner decision). This tool's premise is
                             that bandwidth is not the question; the band is what makes it visible
                             when somebody wants to ask it anyway, and it costs one evaluation per
                             point.

                             IT IS CLAMPED TO THE GENERATOR TABLE'S SPAN WITH A STATED NOTE, never
                             refused — the ONE caller allowed to clamp. A band is a viewing choice,
                             where a design frequency is a design input: extrapolating the generator
                             impedance past the table would be inventing data, and narrowing the
                             view costs the user only the part of the picture that was never
                             there. -->
                        <Border Classes="card">
                            <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                <TextBlock Classes="cardhdr" Text="Swept band"/>

                                <CheckBox MinHeight="0" FontSize="11" Content="Draw band"
                                          IsChecked="{Binding ViewModel.SweepEnabled, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                          ToolTip.Tip="A thin locus through the load points across a band. Clamped to the generator table's span, with the span named in the strip — never refused."/>

                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel" Text="Start"/>
                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val" Margin="4,0,0,0"
                                                        IsEnabled="{Binding ViewModel.SweepEnabled}"
                                                        Text="{Binding ViewModel.SweepStartEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                        ToolTip.Tip="The band's low end. Double-click to edit."/>
                                </Grid>

                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel" Text="Stop"/>
                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val" Margin="4,0,0,0"
                                                        IsEnabled="{Binding ViewModel.SweepEnabled}"
                                                        Text="{Binding ViewModel.SweepStopEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                        ToolTip.Tip="The band's high end. Double-click to edit."/>
                                </Grid>

                                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                    <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel" Text="Points"/>
                                    <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val" Margin="4,0,0,0"
                                                        IsEnabled="{Binding ViewModel.SweepEnabled}"
                                                        Text="{Binding ViewModel.SweepPointsEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                        ToolTip.Tip="How many frequencies the band is walked at. Two is the fewest that draws one."/>
                                </Grid>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Border>
                    </StackPanel>
                </ScrollViewer>
            </Border>

            <GridSplitter Grid.Column="1" Width="4" ResizeDirection="Columns"
                          Background="Transparent"
                          DragCompleted="OnSplitterDragCompleted"/>

            <!-- ── The chart (brief 5) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
                 THE CHART IS THE TOOL and takes the large majority of the area; that proportion is
                 the point of §5.2's sketch and it is set here, by the row and column defaults on the
                 document's View block, rather than by whatever brief 5 happens to drop in. -->
            <Border Grid.Column="2" Classes="pane" Name="ChartRegion" Padding="4">
                <!-- ONE SMITH RENDERER, and this is not a second one (R-smith5-1). The grid, the
                     arcs, the zoom-aware numbering, pan, zoom, marker add/drag/hit-test, the
                     inspector, the context menu, the axis limits and Copy Plot are all this
                     control's own. What the tool adds is the OVERLAY — the grippers, the arrowheads
                     and the load-point labels — and the overlay draws above the trajectories and
                     below the markers, inside PlotRenderer's own draw.

                     CanDeletePlot is False and Plot.IsFixedReadout hides the Add and the per-card
                     trash: every trace here is rebuilt from the design on each edit, so a trace
                     added in the inspector would be gone by the next keystroke and one removed would
                     be back. What the panel CAN change — a colour, a line — is carried across.

                     THE FOUR PROVIDERS AND THE OVERLAY ARE WIRED IN CODE, in BindChartPlot: a
                     PlotControl asks its HOST for the marker index, the info-box view model, the
                     selected markers and the CONTAINER, and a host that is null answers "nothing"
                     to all four, silently. See the code-behind's own note. -->
                <Grid>
                    <ddc:PlotControl Name="ChartPlotControl"
                                     Plot="{Binding ViewModel.ChartPlot}"
                                     Library="{Binding ViewModel.PlotHost.Library}"
                                     CanDeletePlot="False" CanEditPlotProperties="True"/>

                    <!-- ── The constant-Q toggle (owner instruction, 2026-09-19) ────────────────
                         IN THE CHART'S TOP-LEFT CORNER and not in the side panel, because the arcs
                         are a ruler laid over THIS picture and the control for them belongs on it.
                         A square toolbar button with a Q glyph, the schematic editor's own
                         SelectionBtn/ToolActive pair, so "on" reads the way every other latched tool
                         in circuitRF reads.

                         THE VALUE IS NOT HERE. It is drawn on the chart itself, below the apex of
                         the inductive arc, by SmithChartChrome — which is below the firewall, so it
                         travels with every copy, export and headless render of the picture rather
                         than living in a panel that no exported chart carries. Dragging either arc
                         is how it is set; the text follows the arc up and down as it moves. -->
                    <Button Classes="SelectionBtn" Padding="6,3"
                            Classes.ToolActive="{Binding ViewModel.ConstantQEnabled}"
                            Command="{Binding ViewModel.ToggleConstantQCommand}"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Top" Margin="4"
                            ToolTip.Tip="Constant Q — draws the pair of constant-Q arcs. Both branches are one setting: drag either one to change Q, and hold shift to land on an exact quarter. The value is printed under the upper arc.">
                        <!-- THE LETTER, not the glyph (owner instruction, 2026-09-19). Material's
                             AlphaQ is a Q inside a rounded box, which at 16 px on a toolbar reads as
                             an O with a tick rather than as a Q. A TextBlock sized and centred to the
                             same 16x16 box keeps the button square and the row aligned. -->
                        <TextBlock Text="Q" Width="16" Height="16" FontSize="14" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                   TextAlignment="Center" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center" LineHeight="16"/>
                    </Button>
                </Grid>
            </Border>
        </Grid>

        <GridSplitter Grid.Row="1" Height="4" ResizeDirection="Rows"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" Background="Transparent"
                      DragCompleted="OnSplitterDragCompleted"/>

        <!-- ══ The network strip (brief 6) ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             R-smith6-1/-2/-4/-5/-6; docs/design/smith-chart.md §5.5, §5.6.

             ONE SCHEMATIC RENDERER, and this is not a second one. The picture is a projection of the
             element list onto a real SchematicModel (SmithNetworkModel) drawn by SchematicRenderer —
             the renderer the schematic editor draws every frame with. The grid, the glyphs, the label
             roles and their colours, the connected-pin markers, the selection outline and the disabled
             overlay are all its own.

             IT IS A PROJECTION, NOT AN EDITABLE SCHEMATIC. No wire tool, no free placement, no
             selection model beyond "which element is selected" — the topology is a list, and the only
             drag the canvas offers moves an element along the strip, which reorders that list.

             Tall enough for one row of symbols plus the selected element's sliders. -->
        <Border Grid.Row="2" Classes="pane" Name="NetworkRegion" Padding="6">
            <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">

                <!-- The toolbar. Add appends at the end (nearest the load); Insert places before the
                     selection. BOTH MENUS ARE BUILT IN CODE from SmithChartViewModel.ElementMenu —
                     one vocabulary, so the two cannot disagree, and so the shell's own Insert menu can
                     be filled from the same list. -->
                <Grid Grid.Row="0" ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" Margin="0,0,0,4">
                    <TextBlock Classes="panelhdr" Text="Network" Margin="0"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center"/>

                    <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4">
                        <!-- SQUARE ICON BUTTONS, the schematic and layout toolbars' own control
                             (owner instruction, 2026-09-19): Classes="SelectionBtn", Padding 6,3, a
                             16x16 MaterialIcon and no text. The three of them sat beside four that
                             already looked like that, so the row read as two toolbars. Each menu row
                             underneath carries the PART'S OWN GLYPH — see BuildElementMenus. -->
                        <Button Name="AddElementButton" Classes="SelectionBtn" Padding="6,3"
                                ToolTip.Tip="Add — an element at the end of the cascade, nearest the load.">
                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="Plus" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                        </Button>
                        <Button Name="InsertElementButton" Classes="SelectionBtn" Padding="6,3"
                                ToolTip.Tip="Insert — an element before the selected one. With nothing selected this appends, because &quot;before nothing&quot; and &quot;at the end&quot; are the same place in a list.">
                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="TableColumnPlusBefore" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                        </Button>
                        <Button Classes="SelectionBtn" Padding="6,3"
                                Command="{Binding ViewModel.DeleteElementCommand}"
                                ToolTip.Tip="Delete — removes the selected element. The chain closes up.">
                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="Delete" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                        </Button>

                        <!-- Reorder. The arrows are DRAWING-relative and not list-relative: a
                             left-pointing button beside a picture moves the part left, and in a
                             mirrored strip that is toward the load. See MoveElementLeft. -->
                        <Button Classes="SelectionBtn" Padding="6,3"
                                Command="{Binding ViewModel.MoveElementLeftCommand}"
                                ToolTip.Tip="Move the selected element one place to the left. You can also drag it along the strip.">
                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="ChevronLeft" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                        </Button>
                        <Button Classes="SelectionBtn" Padding="6,3"
                                Command="{Binding ViewModel.MoveElementRightCommand}"
                                ToolTip.Tip="Move the selected element one place to the right. You can also drag it along the strip.">
                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="ChevronRight" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                        </Button>

                        <!-- THE MIRROR (R-smith6-6, owner instruction). The schematic editor's own
                             control, taken verbatim — the same glyph its toolbar and the layout
                             editor's already carry for the same idea, so it needs no learning.

                             It mirrors the DRAWING and nothing else: the topology is untouched,
                             element 0 is still the one nearest the generator, and THE CHART DOES NOT
                             MIRROR — the Γ plane's orientation is fixed by physics, inductive above
                             the real axis and capacitive below, so a mirrored Smith chart is a wrong
                             one. There is deliberately no button offering to. -->
                        <Button Classes="SelectionBtn" Padding="6,3"
                                Command="{Binding ViewModel.ToggleMirrorCommand}"
                                ToolTip.Tip="Mirror Network  (M) — flips the drawing so the generator is on the right and the cascade grows leftward. The circuit, the element order and the chart are unchanged.">
                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="FlipHorizontal" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                        </Button>

                        <Button Classes="SelectionBtn" Padding="6,3"
                                Click="OnNetworkZoomToFit"
                                ToolTip.Tip="Zoom to Fit  (F). The strip re-frames itself whenever the drawing changes size — adding, deleting or mirroring — and keeps your zoom when it does not.">
                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="FitToPageOutline" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                        </Button>

                        <!-- HELP (R-smith11-4). railRF, harmonicaRF, wBond and the Match Designer
                             each carry one of these and this window did not, so the chapter written
                             for it was reachable only by knowing it existed. A docked document has
                             no title bar of its own to hang it on, so it goes at the end of the one
                             toolbar this document does have. The destination is DocAnchors' —
                             the docs run fails on a link that resolves to nothing. -->
                        <Button Name="HelpButton" Classes="SelectionBtn" Padding="6,3"
                                Click="OnHelp"
                                ToolTip.Tip="Help — the Smith Chart chapter of the circuitRF reference.">
                            <mi:MaterialIcon Kind="HelpCircleOutline" Width="16" Height="16"/>
                        </Button>
                    </StackPanel>
                </Grid>

                <Border Grid.Row="1" Classes="card" Padding="0" Margin="0,0,0,4" ClipToBounds="True">
                    <sv:SmithNetworkCanvas Name="NetworkCanvas"
                                           ViewModel="{Binding ViewModel}"/>
                </Border>

                <!-- ── The selected element: its name, its Enabled box and its sliders ──────────
                     R-smith6-4's rule is one row per SETTABLE parameter — one for an L, three for an
                     SRLC, two for a TLIN, and NONE AT ALL for an S1P or an S2P, whose value is a
                     file. That case shows the file, its port count and its span instead, and nothing
                     to drag. -->
                <Border Grid.Row="2" Classes="card" Margin="0"
                        IsVisible="{Binding ViewModel.HasSelectedElement}">
                    <StackPanel Spacing="3">

                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,90,*,Auto">
                            <TextBlock Classes="detailLabel" Text="Name" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                            <!-- EVERY editable value is an InlineEditText (R-smith4-5), and the
                                 instance name is one: it is what brief 7 carries into a real
                                 schematic, and a name that collides with another element's is
                                 refused in the strip rather than de-duplicated behind the user. -->
                            <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val" Margin="4,0,0,0"
                                                HorizontalContentAlignment="Left"
                                                Text="{Binding ViewModel.SelectedElementNameEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="Double-click to rename. Names are unique — this is what every refusal, every undo entry and every copied schematic calls the part."/>
                            <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Classes="detailLabel" Margin="8,0,0,0"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                       Text="{Binding ViewModel.SelectedElementHeader}"/>
                            <!-- A disabled element contributes nothing, draws no trajectory, and KEEPS
                                 ITS VALUES AND ITS PLACE. One boolean, and it is the difference
                                 between trying something and losing it. -->
                            <CheckBox Grid.Column="3" FontSize="11" MinHeight="0" Padding="4,0,0,0"
                                      Content="enabled"
                                      IsChecked="{Binding ViewModel.SelectedElementEnabled, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                      ToolTip.Tip="A disabled element contributes nothing and draws no trajectory, and keeps its values and its place in the cascade."/>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- The file kinds: what there is to say instead of a slider. -->
                        <TextBlock Classes="note" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                                   IsVisible="{Binding ViewModel.HasFileSummary}"
                                   Text="{Binding ViewModel.SelectedFileSummary}"
                                   ToolTip.Tip="{Binding ViewModel.SelectedFileSummary}"/>

                        <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding ViewModel.SliderRows}">
                            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                <DataTemplate x:DataType="sm:SmithSliderRowViewModel">
                                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="34,72,*,72,92" Margin="0,1">
                                        <!-- THE ACTIVE ROW IS MARKED (R-smith6-5). The last-touched
                                             slider becomes the element's ActiveParameter, which is
                                             what brief 5's gripper drags — so the connection between
                                             "the slider I just used" and "the handle on the chart" is
                                             visible rather than remembered. Clicking the label makes
                                             it active without moving anything. -->
                                        <Button Classes="rowlabel"
                                                Classes.activeparam="{Binding IsActive}"
                                                Content="{Binding Label}"
                                                Click="OnParameterLabelClick"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="Click to make this the parameter the chart's gripper drags."/>

                                        <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="1" Classes="val"
                                                            HorizontalContentAlignment="Right"
                                                            Text="{Binding MinEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                            ToolTip.Tip="The low end of this slider's range. Double-click to edit."/>

                                        <!-- Compact, and LOGARITHMIC for R, L, C and a line's Z₀:
                                             those span decades, and a linear slider over 1 … 100 pF
                                             spends nine tenths of its travel above 10 pF. -->
                                        <Slider Grid.Column="2" Classes="compact"
                                                Minimum="{Binding Minimum}" Maximum="{Binding Maximum}"
                                                Value="{Binding Position, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                Loaded="OnNetworkSliderLoaded"/>

                                        <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="3" Classes="val"
                                                            HorizontalContentAlignment="Left"
                                                            Text="{Binding MaxEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                            ToolTip.Tip="The high end of this slider's range. Double-click to edit."/>

                                        <!-- Typing a value outside the range RE-CENTRES the range
                                             rather than clamping: a typed number is an instruction,
                                             not a gesture. -->
                                        <ctl:InlineEditText Grid.Column="4" Classes="val" Margin="6,0,0,0"
                                                            Text="{Binding ValueEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                            ToolTip.Tip="Double-click to type a value with its unit. A value outside the slider's range moves the range rather than being clamped into it."/>
                                    </Grid>
                                </DataTemplate>
                            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                        </ItemsControl>
                    </StackPanel>
                </Border>
            </Grid>
        </Border>

        <!-- The marker info-box layer (R-smith5-5's last clause).

             MARKERS EXISTED BEFORE A PANEL COULD HOST THEIR BOXES once already — railRF shipped that
             round — so this window hosts them from the start. A box is DRAGGABLE and belongs to the
             display rather than to the plot it was made from, so the layer spans the WHOLE document
             and not the chart pane: a box pinned inside the pane could not be moved off its own
             curve. Placed last so it renders above everything, ROWSPAN across all four rows.

             Background="{x:Null}" on both the ItemsControl and its Canvas: a null background is not
             hit-testable, so every click falls through to the chart underneath, while each
             MarkerInfoBoxView sets Background="Transparent" and is hit-testable itself.

             SmithChartView.SyncPlotContainer keeps the container's Left/Top/Width/Height equal to the
             PlotControl's rectangle IN THIS LAYER'S COORDINATES, which is the space ViewLeft/ViewTop
             are in. -->
        <ItemsControl Name="MarkerInfoBoxLayer" Grid.Row="0" Grid.RowSpan="4"
                      ItemsSource="{Binding ViewModel.PlotHost.MarkerInfoBoxes}"
                      Background="{x:Null}">
            <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <Canvas Background="{x:Null}"/>
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
            <ItemsControl.ItemContainerTheme>
                <ControlTheme TargetType="ContentPresenter">
                    <Setter Property="Canvas.Left" Value="{ReflectionBinding ViewLeft}"/>
                    <Setter Property="Canvas.Top"  Value="{ReflectionBinding ViewTop}"/>
                </ControlTheme>
            </ItemsControl.ItemContainerTheme>
            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate x:DataType="ddv:MarkerInfoBoxViewModel">
                    <ddvw:MarkerInfoBoxView/>
                </DataTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
        </ItemsControl>

        <!-- ══ The status strip (R-smith4-8) ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             ALWAYS ON SCREEN, and it STATES NUMBERS. Every one of them is the number the evaluator
             produced, formatted by MatchValueFormat — never re-derived for display, because a second
             spelling of "the load impedance" is a second answer nobody can tell apart from the first.

             A refusal appears here WITH ITS NUMBERS IN IT and turns the offending input red; the two
             are one mechanism, not a sentence plus a separate hunt. It replaces the reading rather
             than sitting beside it: two rows of text, one of them in the warning colour, read as two
             different problems. -->
        <Border Grid.Row="3" Classes="pane" Margin="4,0,4,4" Padding="10,4">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*">
                <SelectableTextBlock Name="StatusStrip" Classes="note" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                     Text="{Binding ViewModel.StatusLine}"
                                     IsVisible="{Binding ViewModel.ShowStatusLine}"/>
                <SelectableTextBlock Name="RefusalStrip" Classes="warn" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                     Text="{Binding ViewModel.Refusal}"
                                     IsVisible="{Binding ViewModel.HasRefusal}"/>
                <!-- A NOTE is the third state of this one line, and it REPLACES the reading for the
                     same reason a refusal does: two rows of text read as two different problems. It
                     says what the tool decided rather than what is wrong — a line's reference
                     frequency left behind by a retune, a name already taken, a file element nobody
                     finished choosing a file for — and it is cleared by the next edit. -->
                <SelectableTextBlock Name="NoticeStrip" Classes="note" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                     Text="{Binding ViewModel.StripNotice}"
                                     IsVisible="{Binding ViewModel.HasStripNotice}"/>
            </Grid>
        </Border>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
